WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.830
- We've got to say the magic words,

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:03.660
otherwise it's not gonna work.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:05.790
- [All] Light as a
feather, stiff as a board.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.950
Light as a feather, stiff as a board.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:10.017
Light as a feather, stiff as a board.

00:00:10.017 --> 00:00:11.820
(group laughing)

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.800
- Chances are if you've
been to a sleepover,

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.210
you've heard ghost stories,

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:16.740
tried to contact the dead,

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:20.040
read fortunes, and attempted
to make your friends levitate.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:21.600
Bloody Mary, Ouija,

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.270
light as a feather stiff
as a board, concentrate,

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:25.710
they're scary and entertaining,

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:26.850
and participating in them

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:28.920
can be a right of passage for children

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:30.540
and maybe even adults.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.700
But do you know the histories behind them?

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.110
Do they actually work?

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.040
And further more,

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.470
why do we love to scare
each other so much?

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.173
So join us for one hell
of a slumber party.

00:00:43.439 --> 00:00:44.587
(phone ringing)

00:00:44.587 --> 00:00:45.420
- [Person On Phone] Hello?

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.965
- Hey, do you wanna come to a sleepover?

00:00:47.965 --> 00:00:49.380
(dramatic music)

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:53.400
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka,
and this is "Monstrum."

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:54.750
The concept of a slumber party

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.850
has been around since the 19th century,

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:58.590
when private bedrooms became the norm

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:01.560
and group sleeping became
more fun than function.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.480
The sleepover was solidified
as a social institution

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.800
when it was recognized in
newspapers and society journals.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.090
In the early 20th century,

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:11.460
the sleepover turned a bit more spooky.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.080
According to "The Mary Dawson Game Book,"

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.840
the slumber party was the
latest and most popular affair

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:16.980
for a young girl.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.690
The book describes the ideal sleepover

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:20.580
as hosted in a bedroom with a fireplace

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.020
adding to the atmosphere

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.810
to inspire spooky stories
and fortune telling.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:26.700
The sleepover became a right of passage,

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.550
at least for Americans,
by the end of the 1920s.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.160
Particularly for girls and young women.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.470
And they still have that reputation today.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.140
At early sleepovers, communal
fire lights set the scene

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.240
for what would become a
fundamental ingredient,

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:40.650
spooky games.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:43.080
A good ghost story could
win a girl a prize.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.300
Palms would be read and fortunes told.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.340
Driftwood powder would
be thrown on the flames

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.470
to produce all the colors of the rainbow.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:52.590
A bit of science disguised as magic.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:54.720
- Driftwood, especially
if it's from the ocean,

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.340
will burn in different colors.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.860
Mostly shades of blue and light purple,

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.320
due to the presence of
absorbed metal salts,

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.900
things like copper oxide
and copper sulfate.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:05.190
When you burn the wood,

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:08.160
you're adding energy to
the atoms in the salts.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:11.730
They can lose that energy
again by releasing light.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:13.650
Different elements give
off different colors

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.390
just like fireworks,

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.070
where the specific mix of ingredients,

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:20.730
many of them metals, create
a whole rainbow of bursts.

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:23.460
But don't try this
driftwood magic at home.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.725
It is literally toxic.

00:02:25.725 --> 00:02:27.690
- Levitation games or tricks

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:29.070
have been a pastime for children

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:30.720
for literally hundreds of years.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:31.800
No, really.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.480
Records from the 17th century

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.540
note this curiosity in discussion
of children's folklore,

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.060
and the first written
mention of levitation games

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:41.490
appears in "The Diary Of Samuel Pepys."

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:44.130
It details a spell performed
by four French girls

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:47.160
as they lifted a little boy
in a pseudo resurrection.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:48.510
In the mid 14th century,

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:51.120
a horrific plague, many
today know as the Black Death

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:52.620
swept through the region.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.390
What may have begun as a way for kids

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:56.010
to confront the death that surrounded them

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:57.390
during times of plague

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:00.270
and a mass death endured
as a liminal game.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.160
You probably know what this looks like.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:03.960
Participants lay only a couple of fingers

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:06.090
on the resurrectee and chant.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:09.720
In 1843, Sir David Brewster's
letters on natural magic

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:11.430
addressed to Sir Walter Scott

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:13.170
described the ritual with a phrase

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:15.877
that would give rise to a
part of the well known chant.

00:03:15.877 --> 00:03:18.397
"To his own surprise
and that of his bearers,

00:03:18.397 --> 00:03:20.407
"he rises with the greatest facility,

00:03:20.407 --> 00:03:23.077
"as if he were no heavier than a feather."

00:03:23.077 --> 00:03:24.630
- I couldn't give her the leverage.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:25.801
- Yeah.

00:03:25.801 --> 00:03:27.300
(Emily laughing)

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.430
- Okay, I'm trying not to laugh.

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:30.603
- Nope.
- No.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:32.423
- In reality, of course,

00:03:32.423 --> 00:03:35.130
the person is not suddenly
as light as a feather,

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:36.510
but instead they weigh exactly the same

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:38.280
as what gravity says they do.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:39.690
When the trick works,

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:41.010
it's simply because the weight of the body

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:44.190
is distributed among four
people, and eight hands,

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:47.400
you're not lifting a 60 kilo
person with your fingers,

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:48.930
you're just lifting 15 kilos,

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:50.730
which is very within reason.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:54.000
And often, those attempting
the light as a feather ritual,

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.020
they try to lift the body

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:58.530
without saying the magic
words, then they fail.

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:02.076
But when the ritual words
are repeated, the lift works.

00:04:02.076 --> 00:04:04.456
- [All] Light as a
feather, stiff as a board.

00:04:04.456 --> 00:04:06.501
Light as a feather, stiff as a board.

00:04:06.501 --> 00:04:08.523
Light as a feather, stiff as a board.

00:04:08.523 --> 00:04:10.375
Light as a feather, stiff as a board.

00:04:10.375 --> 00:04:11.640
Light as a feather, stiff as a board.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.350
- The words hold no magic

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:16.110
beyond unconsciously
signaling everyone to lift

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:18.270
at precisely the same time.

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:21.510
A spookier version of
counting down three, two, one.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:23.640
This is an illusion of control,

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:25.860
where our brains assume
a cause and effect link

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:29.490
for two events that simply
just happen close in time.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:31.590
- And centuries later,
kids are still trying

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:33.600
to levitate their friends with chants.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.570
Why do kids want to scare
each other at parties?

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:38.760
The communal aspect of
these games and rituals

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:40.350
is an important facet.

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:42.720
Not only are participants
bonding when they are pushed

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:44.880
outside of their emotional comfort zones,

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:47.610
they offer opportunities
to demonstrate maturity

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:50.100
by showing courage in the face of fear.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.330
They're a rite of passage,

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:53.703
an event that marks transition or growth.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:56.430
- Rite comes from the Latin ritus,

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:59.250
meaning religious observance,
ceremony, or behavior.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:01.200
And the phrase rite of passage,

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.050
yeah, much more recent, the
turn of the 20th century.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:06.540
The same time the
sleepover really took off.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:07.830
Coincidence?

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:08.850
I don't think so.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:11.310
The term is generally credited
to French ethnographer

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:13.200
and folklorist, Arnold Van Gennep,

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:16.110
who in 1909 argued that
the ceremonial events

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:18.510
involved a sequence of
three distinct parts.

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:21.150
Separation, transition, and incorporation.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:23.340
Sleepover games, albeit loosely,

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.500
under the hallmarks of this definition.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.000
Separating from the familiar,

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.040
both one's home and comfort level.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:31.920
A transition, the participation
and the spooky test

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:33.390
or celebrated ritual.

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:36.750
And finally, reincorporation
back into one's own home

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:38.250
at the end of the sleepover.

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:40.380
A liminal space is an in between one,

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:42.720
familiar, but not entirely comfortable.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.130
There's a level of discomfort

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:46.290
sleeping in a space that's not your own.

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:48.330
And sleepovers occur first and frequently

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:50.580
during a liminal age, puberty.

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:51.900
Which is terrifying enough

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:53.910
without adding all the scary stories.

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:55.500
And while under adult supervision,

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:57.060
they are peer run environments

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:00.150
that promote activities not
allowed in every day life.

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:02.250
Granting children an
opportunity to explore

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:03.660
their own identities.

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:05.430
Eating marshmallows until you get sick,

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:08.400
staying up way past bedtime,
and summoning ghosts,

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.500
yeah, not usually a kid's daily routine.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:12.390
Sleepovers aren't all about rebellion,

00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:15.420
but curiosity, exploration,
and independence.

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:16.710
On the cusp of adulthood,

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:19.200
many children and teens
want to test the boundaries

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.720
of what is known, literally
and metaphysically.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:22.950
One ritualistic practice

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:25.770
that really test boundaries is
communicating with the dead,

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:28.290
or trying to, at least in my experience.

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:31.500
And fun fact, while the slumber
party was gaining popularity

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:32.640
in the 19th century,

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:35.700
so was another social trend, spiritualism.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:39.150
In 1848, young teen
sisters, Kate and Maggie Fox

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:40.650
developed a cult following

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:43.290
for their reported ability
to communicate with the dead,

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:45.210
via a coded wrapping technique.

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:46.380
They took their act on tour,

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:49.230
demonstrating their abilities
to large paying audiences,

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:52.080
ushering the spiritualist
movement into popular culture.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:53.700
Participants in the spiritualist movement

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:55.860
believe the soul lived on past death

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:57.900
and tried to seek guidance
from the spirit world

00:06:57.900 --> 00:06:59.400
at small group occasions,

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.290
often led by an employed medium.

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:02.970
While attempting to
communicate with the deceased

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:05.040
is nothing new in human history,

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:06.660
spiritualism took these practices

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:09.090
out of religious or traditional ceremonies

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:10.890
and into the popular imagination.

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:13.830
Drawing rooms, public
halls, theaters, camps,

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:15.510
and other leisure spaces,

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:17.550
joined believers and skeptics together

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:19.800
to witness communication
into the afterlife.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:20.880
Objects might levitate,

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:23.640
spirits could leave their
impression on photographic plates,

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:26.460
whisper in the medium's
ear, or tap on a table.

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:28.100
There were certainly true believers

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:29.340
in the spiritualist movement,

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:30.960
but even for the worst skeptical,

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.300
the experience itself was important.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:36.120
That collective fear experience
that offered the possibility

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:38.340
of an inexplicable mysterious event

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:40.230
was too tantalizing to miss.

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:42.180
People who didn't have access to mediums

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:44.040
would engage in other small rituals

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:45.570
attempting to communicate with the dead,

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:46.860
or see into the future.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:48.720
Cue Bloody Mary.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:50.670
If you've seen "Monstrum's"
Blood Mary episode,

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:53.280
you already know that in
the early 20th century,

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:55.500
mirror divination rituals were popular,

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:57.780
ramping up during the Halloween season.

00:07:57.780 --> 00:07:59.670
Young adults, particularly women,

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:01.710
turned to the mirror
to see into the future,

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:05.130
often with the intention of
identifying their future spouse.

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:07.410
The Bloody Mary variation
is one of the simplest,

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:09.240
as it only requires three things,

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:11.490
a dark room, a mirror, and a chant.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:13.320
I'm trying not to blink,

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:15.660
and I'm trying to make
eye contact with myself,

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:17.550
so I feel like I'm seeing something here.

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:19.050
- [Alex] Oh, out of the corner...

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:19.883
- Oh, god, the TV.

00:08:19.883 --> 00:08:22.053
No!
- You unplugged it.

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:24.360
- That's...

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:25.193
- [Joe] What happened?

00:08:25.193 --> 00:08:26.026
- No, it didn't.

00:08:26.026 --> 00:08:27.630
- That was an unplugged TV.

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:30.060
- Tools some spiritualist
use to connect to the dead

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:32.250
also made their way into
sleepover tradition,

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:34.050
like the Ouija Board.

00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:37.500
In February 1891, Baltimore
inventor, Elijah J. Bond

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:40.140
patented a toy, or game, called Ouija,

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:41.970
or Egyptian Luck Board.

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:44.280
Supposedly, he named
the game on the advice

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:46.170
of his sister-in-law, a medium,

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:48.540
who asked the board what
it wanted to be called.

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:49.860
It spelled Ouija.

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:52.320
Talking boards, simple
boards with printed letters

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:55.230
predate the Ouija, and served
essentially the same purpose,

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:56.760
the communicate with spirits.

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:58.050
However, Bond's intent

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:00.270
wasn't a means of spirit communication.

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:02.760
The patent says that the
player's touch of the hand

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:04.350
is what moves the planchette.

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:05.183
Capitalism.

00:09:05.183 --> 00:09:06.810
This was not the only parlor game

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:08.370
marketed to communicate with the dead,

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:10.350
but it would become the most popular.

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:12.390
Ouija really took off in the 20th century,

00:09:12.390 --> 00:09:15.210
when it began appearing
in pop culture texts.

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:17.640
In 1920, a book, "Our Unseen Guest,"

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:19.440
followed the story of a married couple

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:22.440
who began to communicate
with a World War I soldier

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:25.320
after they discovered a
Ouija Board on Halloween.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:28.110
Anonymously published and
marketed as nonfiction,

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:31.650
it contains two letters
dictated by the spirit himself.

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:33.540
In 1990, the authors were revealed

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:36.750
to be two newspaper reporters,
and closet spiritualists.

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:39.780
But it was a 1973 horror
movie that would skyrocket

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:43.680
the Ouija Board's notoriety
and popularity, "The Exorcist."

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:45.955
- Hey, where'd this come from?

00:09:45.955 --> 00:09:48.690
- The movie adapts William
Peter Blatty's novel

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:50.550
about a young girl possessed by a demon

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:51.960
after playing with a Ouija Board

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:53.430
she discovers in the basement.

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:55.170
Other pop culture references to Ouija

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:57.210
frequently show the gameplay collectively,

00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:59.340
sometimes a game
contributes more to comedy,

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:01.410
like in "Sugar And Spice," or "Friends,"

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:04.500
but more often, it's evoked
to help scare the audience,

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:06.547
like in "Ouija," "Thirteen Ghosts,"

00:10:06.547 --> 00:10:08.580
"The Conjuring 2," or "Veronica."

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:11.190
While legends like Bloody
Mary are evoked in ritual,

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:13.230
these are done primarily to insight fear

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:15.300
rather than actually summon a spirit.

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:18.300
A Ouija Board blurs the line
between occult practices

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:20.490
and playful evocation of terror.

00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:21.600
Some scholars argue

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:24.870
that publicly vocal fundamentalist
Christians in the 1970s

00:10:24.870 --> 00:10:27.720
along with the Satanic Panic of the 1980s

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:29.490
contributed to a shift in perspective

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:32.220
of these so called occult sleepover games.

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:34.650
Both movements attributed
any dabbling in the occult

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:36.240
as inviting in the devil,

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:38.670
associating such practices
with the demonic.

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:40.620
- [Erica] Well, you can actually...

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:41.453
No.

00:10:41.453 --> 00:10:42.286
- Oh my god.

00:10:42.286 --> 00:10:43.710
- Wait, what?

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:44.982
What was it, you?

00:10:44.982 --> 00:10:46.565
- It has to be Joe.
- You're lying.

00:10:46.565 --> 00:10:48.200
- It's 100% Joe.

00:10:48.200 --> 00:10:49.866
- Your smile, it's definitely you.

00:10:49.866 --> 00:10:51.439
- [Joe] 'Cause one of
you is messing with it.

00:10:51.439 --> 00:10:52.272
- I'm not doing it.

00:10:52.272 --> 00:10:53.400
Whether you believe or not,

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:55.440
it's hard to deny the omnipresent reach

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:56.940
of the Ouija game today,

00:10:56.940 --> 00:10:58.410
with the board's familiar lettering

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:01.800
appearing on rugs,
shirts, mints, fake nails,

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:03.720
and throw blankets everywhere.

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:05.220
Nervous to dabble in the spirit world

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:06.660
for answers about the future?

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:08.610
You could always try concentrate.

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:10.950
This trance game in meant
to predict how you will die

00:11:10.950 --> 00:11:13.980
and involves a really creepy poem/ritual

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:16.080
designed to give someone the chills.

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:18.780
The origins of this one are very vague,

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:21.000
and no, Dwight did not invent it.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:23.850
My best guess is it developed
during the mid 20th century

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:25.950
as a messed up form of guided meditation

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:27.120
meant to scare people.

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:29.100
Fun fact, I remember
playing this as a kid,

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:32.490
not at a sleepover, but during
recess at my Catholic school.

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:35.010
Supposedly, the color you
see after being pushed

00:11:35.010 --> 00:11:37.050
indicates how you will die in the future.

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:39.450
More than trying to experience
a supernatural event,

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:41.580
these rituals are about
creating connection

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:44.490
with the other participants
and testing boundaries.

00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:47.040
Fear can challenge us and traumatize us,

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:48.900
but it can also bond us.

00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:50.820
Strange games and liminal spaces

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:53.670
allow us to confront the
unknown in safe environments,

00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:56.733
preparing us for the many
unexpected mysteries of life.

00:12:04.696 --> 00:12:06.780
Before you go, I wanted to remind you

00:12:06.780 --> 00:12:07.650
that a new season

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:10.440
of our sister show, "Fate
&amp; Fabled" is underway.

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:13.080
Join me and Dr. McTier
for an all new series

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:14.880
of the wildest, most adventurous,

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:16.710
and sometimes weirdest stories

00:12:16.710 --> 00:12:19.440
from our strange world's
folklore and mythologies,

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:21.300
right here on Storied.

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:23.820
But it was a 1973 horror movie

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:26.160
that would skyrocket the Ouija's Board...

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:27.720
The wigi board.

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:28.860
Luigi board.

00:12:28.860 --> 00:12:29.910
So evocation is right?

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:31.023
- Yeah.
- Aw.

